    "ls|LS17_S1_U_260": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 260 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.06.2019 \n \nPRADHAN MANTRI KAUSHAL VIKAS YOJANA \n \n260. SHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE: DR. SUBHASH RAMR AO BHAMRE: \n DR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE:  SHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA: \n DR. HEENA GAVIT:    SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \n \nWill the MINISTER of SKILL DEVELOPMENT and ENTREPRE NEURSHIP \nbe pleased to state : \n \n(a) the details of targets set, achievements/progre ss made under the Pradhan \nMantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) during the last  three years and the current \nyear, State/UT-wise; \n \n(b) the details of the funds allocated / released a nd utilized for the Yojana along \nwith the number of persons benefited/ trained there under during each of the last \nthree years and the current year, State/UTwise; \n \n(c) the details of Skill Development Centres under the Yojana presently \nfunctioning in the country, State/UT-wise; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to open such ce ntres under the Yojana in \neach district of the country and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(e) whether the Government has reviewed the Yojana to ascertain its \nshortcomings and the number of persons who got empl oyment thereunder and if so, \nthe details thereof; and \n \n(f) the further measures taken by the Government to  make the Yojana more \neffective for its better implementation? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R. K. SINGH) \n \n(a) and (b) Under the Skill India Mission, Ministry  of Skill Development and \nEntrepreneurship (MSDE) is implementing a flagship scheme called the Pradhan \nMantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) 2016-20 with an  objective to impart skill \ntraining to 1 crore prospective youth pan India for  four years with a budgetary outlay \nof Rs. 12,000 crore. PMKVY has two components known  as Centrally Sponsored \nCentrally Managed (CSCM) being implemented by Natio nal Skill Development \nCorporation (NSDC) and Centrally Sponsored State Ma naged (CSSM) being \nimplemented by State Skill Development Missions of the States/ UTs popularly \nknown as State- Engagement Component of PMKVY (2016-20). \n \nUnder PMKVY 2016-20, as on 12.06.2019, 52.12 lakh ( appx.) candidates \nhave been trained (31.08 lakh STT + 21.04 lakh RPL)  under STT and RPL in the \ncountry. The State/UT-wise details of trained candidates are given at Annexure I . \n \nUnder CSCM component of PMKVY 2016-20, there is no provision of State-\nwise funds allocation. As on 12.06.2019, funds amou nting to Rs. 3,987.16 Cr. have \nbeen disbursed to implementing agency (i.e. NSDC) f or implementation of the \nscheme throughout the country. However, under CSSM component of PMKVY 2016-\n\n20, funds, and corresponding physical targets have been allocated to the States for \nthe implementation of scheme through State Skill De velopment Missions. The State-\nwise details of funds disbursed under CSSM componen t of PMKVY 2016-20, as on \n12.06.2019, are given at Annexure-II.  \n \n(c) and (d) PMKVY 2016-20 does not mandate the esta blishment of skill \ndevelopment centres, however, training has been imp arted through accredited and \naffiliated training centers (TCs). Under the scheme , the accreditation and affiliation of \nTCs are being done under single window IT applicati on known as SMART. As on \n12.06.2019, 12,127 TCs have been empanelled under PMKVY 2016-20. \n \nFurther, Ministry also promotes establishment of mo del and aspirational skill \ncentres known as Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Kendra (PMK K) in every district for \nimparting skill training through PMKVY 2016-20. As on date, 837 PMKKs have been \nallocated across the country covering 717 districts  and 543 Parliamentary \nConstituencies. Out of allocated PMKKs, 610 PMKKs have been already established. \n \nThe State/UT – wise numbers of empanelled TCs under  PMKVY 2016-20 and \nestablished PMKKs are given at Annexure III . \n \n(e) and (f) Appraisal of the on-going schemes and c onsequent improvements is an \nongoing process. MSDE has taken various measures an d interventions for better \nimplementation of the scheme such as Direct Benefit  Transfer, Aadhaar linkage of \ntrainers and trainees, Aadhaar enabled biometric at tendance of candidate and \ntrainers, maintaining of all MIS through SDMS porta l to check duplication of \ncandidates, etc. PMKVY 2016-20 has provisions relat ing to continuous as well as \nperformance monitoring of training centre. For ensu ring quality of training under the \nscheme, all accreditation and affiliation of traini ng centre is done through an online \nportal SMART with inspections done by an independen t third party assessment \nagency. Adding to the improved accreditation proces s, MSDE has initiated a process \nof continuous monitoring through various channels a nd mandatory re-accreditation \nevery year. Under this, self-audit reporting, call validations, surprise visits and \nmonitoring through the Skills Development Managemen t System (SDMS) have been \nadopted. Further, the targets are being assigned to  the TCs with a provision for \nperiodic review. The grading is linked to quality o f training, infrastructure availability, \ntraining capacity, past performance, geographical l ocation and other relevant \nparameters. \n \nMoreover, under PMKVY 2016-20 scheme, placement tra cking is mandatory. \nThe placement data is reported within 90 days of certification of trained candidate. As \nper data reported on SDMS, as on 12.06.2019, 24.56 lakh candidates are certified \nunder STT of PMKVY 2016-20. The number of candidate s certified under STT of \nPMKVY 90 days prior i.e. 14.03.2019 is 23.28 lakh. Out of these candidates, as on \n12.06.2019, 12.6 lakh candidates have been placed i n various sectors across the \ncountry which is 54.12 % (approx.) of the certified candidates. \n   \n\nAnnexure I \n \nThe State/UT-wise details of trained candidates, as on 12.06.2019, are as under: \n \nS. \nNo.  \nState Name  Trained  \n(STT + RPL) \n1.  Andaman and Nicobar Island 215  \n2.  Andhra Pradesh 150605  \n3.  Arunachal Pradesh 9636  \n4.  Assam 136476  \n5.  Bihar 233947  \n6.  Chandigarh 9917  \n7.  Chhattisgarh 74225  \n8.  Dadra and Nagar Haveli 2426  \n9.  Daman and Diu 2566  \n10.  Delhi 199831  \n11.  Goa 3495  \n12.  Gujarat 157099  \n13.  Haryana 356993  \n14.  Himachal Pradesh 55766  \n15.  Jammu and Kashmir 111547  \n16.  Jharkhand 92610  \n17.  Karnataka 235715  \n18.  Kerala 126341  \n19.  Madhya Pradesh 389233  \n20.  Maharashtra 347831  \n21.  Manipur 21525  \n22.  Meghalaya 14325  \n23.  Mizoram 5662  \n24.  Nagaland 4595  \n25.  Odisha 190857  \n26.  Puducherry 12584  \n27.  Punjab 190606  \n28.  Rajasthan 418121  \n29.  Sikkim 4050  \n30.  Tamil Nadu 351551  \n31.  Telangana 176216  \n32.  Tripura 24351  \n33.  Uttar Pradesh 793816  \n34.  Uttarakhand 89207  \n35.  West Bengal 217990  \nGrand Total  5211930  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure II \n \nThe State-wise details of funds disbursed under CSS M component of PMKVY \n2016-20, as on 12.06.2019, are as under: \n \nS. No  State/UT Name  Funds Released  \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Islands                2,10,78,76 7  \n2 Andhra Pradesh  28,84,26,464  \n3 Arunachal Pradesh  7,21,32,216  \n4 Assam 36,95,32,800  \n5 Bihar 36,81,62,449  \n6 Chandigarh 6,15,88,800  \n7 Chhattisgarh 35,57,76,000  \n8 Dadra and Nagar Haveli 1,10,85,984  \n9 Daman and Diu 3,00,24,540  \n10  Delhi 15,39,72,000  \n11 Goa 10,70,25,937  \n12 Gujarat 35,94,93,826  \n13 Haryana 21,56,99,375  \n14  Himachal Pradesh 21,55,60,800  \n15  Jammu and Kashmir 22,94,18,280  \n16  Jharkhand 29,59,64,978  \n17 Karnataka 21,43,95,135  \n18 Kerala 22,00,25,988  \n19 Lakshadweep 1,23,17,760  \n20  Madhya Pradesh 21,46,66,296  \n21  Maharashtra 85,77,62,615  \n22  Manipur 24,99,88,939  \n23  Meghalaya 12,77,96,760  \n24 Mizoram 10,88,73,601  \n25 Nagaland 16,94,76,980  \n26 Odisha 27,71,49,600  \n27  Puducherry 2,59,55,280  \n28  Punjab 26,39,52,000  \n29  Rajasthan 14,19,35,789  \n30  Sikkim 2,00,16,360  \n31 Tamil Nadu 34,43,10,720  \n32 Telangana 22,94,64,472  \n33 Tripura 8,37,68,100  \n34  Uttar Pradesh 52,26,00,000  \n35  Uttarakhand 20,32,43,040  \n36  West Bengal 38,04,64,812  \nGrand Total 7,82,31,07,463  \n \n  \n\nAnnexure III \n \nThe State-wise numbers of empanelled TCs under PMKV Y 2016-20 and \nestablished PMKKs, as on 12.06.2019, are given below: \n \nS.N.  State/UT  Empanelled TCs  Established PMKKs  \n1. Andaman & Nicobar Islands 6  1 \n2. Andhra Pradesh 351  21  \n3. Arunachal Pradesh 45  0 \n4. Assam 280  20  \n5. Bihar 424  43  \n6. Chandigarh 37  1 \n7. Chhattisgarh 162  26  \n8. Dadra & Nagar Haveli 6  1 \n9. Daman and Diu 11  1 \n10 . Delhi 344  6 \n11 . Goa 18  1 \n12 . Gujarat 358  32  \n13 . Haryana 1003  23  \n14 . Himachal Pradesh 224  10  \n15 . Jammu & Kashmir 343  12  \n16 . Jharkhand 145  22  \n17 . Karnataka 205  34  \n18 . Kerala 241  9 \n19 . Madhya Pradesh 899  45  \n20 . Maharashtra 469  34  \n21 . Manipur 73  4 \n22 . Meghalaya 37  3 \n23 . Mizoram 61  0 \n24 . Nagaland 35  2 \n25 . Odisha 339  25  \n26 . Puducherry 37  1 \n27 . Punjab 637  24  \n28 . Rajasthan 1324  33  \n29 . Sikkim 28  0 \n30 . Tamil Nadu 888  28  \n31 . Telangana 369  16  \n32 . Tripura 126  2 \n33 . Uttar Pradesh 1988  76  \n34 . Uttarakhand 238  13  \n35 . West Bengal 376  41  \n  Grand Total  12127  610  \n \n \n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S1_U_261": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.261 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.06.2019 \n \nSTATUS OF ADARSH SMARAK SCHEME \n \n261. DR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE: \nDR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE: \nSHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE: \nSHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA: \nDR. HEENA GAVIT: \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:  \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the present status of Adarsh Smarak Scheme being implemented by  \nArchaeological Survey of India(ASI); \n(b) the number of protected monuments identified by the ASI to implement  \nthe said scheme, State/UT-wise; \n(c) the details of funds allocated to implement the scheme; \n(d) whether the Adarsh Smarak Scheme has achieved the objective for which  \nit was introduced  and if so, the details thereof; \n(e) whether the Government proposes to upgrade all the monuments managed  \nby ASI to the standards of the Adarsh Smarak in a phased manner and if so,  \nthe details thereof along with the steps taken/being taken in this regard; \n(f) the number of requests received from States/UTs for inclusion of  \ncertain monuments under the said scheme, State/UT -wise along with the  \nstatus of such requests; and \n(g) the other steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR CULTURE AND TOURISM \n \n(PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a)to(c) There is no such specific Adarsh Smarak Scheme being implemented by \nArchaeological Survey of India.  However, 100 numbers of monuments have \nbeen identified as Adarsh Smarak  by the Archaeological Survey of India for \ndevelopment of public amenities. The list of Adarsh Smarak is at Annexure.  \nThere is no separate budget allocation for  Adarsh Smarak and the expenditure \nis met from regular budget allocation of  Archaeological Survey of India. \n(d) At most of the monuments facilities/a menities have either been upgrad ed or \nprovided.  \n(e) No, Sir. However, provision of facilities is always based on need at monument \ndespite its Adarsh status/other monuments.  \n(f)&(g) Since identifying Adarsh Smarak is not a scheme therefore the State \nparticipation does not arise.  \n \n\nANNEXURE  \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a)TO( c) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 261 FOR 24.6.2019 \n \nLIST OF CENTRALLY PROTECTED MONUMENTS IDENTIFIED AS ADARSH \nSMARAK \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of Monument  State \n1 Virabhadra Temple, Lepakshi Dist. Anantpur  Andhra Pradesh \n2 Nagarjunakonda \n3 Bhuddist Remains, Salihundum  \n4 Rang Ghar, Sibsagar  Assam \n5 Vaishali – Kolhua  Bihar \n6 Sher Shah’s Tomb, Sasaram \n7 Excavated Remains at Nalanda      \n8 Lakshman Temple, Sirpur, Dist. Mahasamund  Chhattisgarh \n9 Humayun’s Tomb  Delhi \n10 Red Fort  \n11 Qutb Complex  \n12 Safdarjung Tomb  \n13 Purana Qila  \n14 Church of St. Augustine, Old Goa  Goa \n15 Rani-Ki-Vav, Patan  Gujarat \n16 Champaner Monument, Champaner  \n17 Archaeological Remains of  a Harappan Port Town, Lothal  \n18 Diu Fort  \n19 Dholavira  \n20 Sun Temple, Modhera \n21 Shikh Chilli’s Tomb  Haryana \n22 Jal Mahal, Narnual  \n23 Masrur Rock Cut Temple  Himachal Pradesh \n24 Martand Temple , Kashmir  Jammu & Kashmir \n25 Leh Palace, Leh  \n26 Pari Mahal, Srinagar  \n27 Ram Nagar Palace  \n28 Ancient Tank and excavated remains, Benisagar, Dist. \nSinghbhumi  \nJharkhand \n29 Group of monuments at Hampi  Karnataka \n30 Group of Temples, Pattdakal  \n31 Dariya Daulat Bagh, Shrirangpattnam  \n32 Group of Temples, Aihole  \n33 Fort Bidar, Dist. Bidar  \n34 Shravanbelgola  \n35 Gol- Gumbaz, Bijapur  \n36 Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas, Belur & Helebidu   \n37 Bekal Fort  Kerala \n38 Kudakaliu Parabmu, Thrissur  \n39 St. Anjelo Fort, Kannur, Kerala  \n40 St. Francis Church, Cochin  \n\n41 Western Group of Temples, Khajuraho  Madhya Pradesh \n42 Rayal Palace, Mandu  \n43 Budhhist Monuments at Sanchi  \n44 Pre-historic rock shelters of Bhimbetka  \n45 Group of Temples at Amarkan