    "ls|LS17_S1_U_323": "                   \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION & FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n323\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE   16\nTH\n \nJULY, 2019\n \n \n \n \nSTORAGE FACILITIES\n \n \n*323. \n    \nSHRI PARTHIBAN S.R.: \n \n             \nSHRI DEVUSINH JESINGBHAI CHAUHAN: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE\n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether there is a shortage of storage and cold storage facilities in the country; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof indicating the total available capacity and shortage of such \nfacilities along with the number of cold storage units in public, private and \ncooperative \nsectors, sector and State/UT\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government provides financial assistance to States to set up these \nfacilities and if so, the details of the amount allocated, sanctioned and disbursed to the \nStates during the last two years\n \nand the current year, year and State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(d) \n \nthe schemes being implemented by the Government to provide incentives to \nentrepreneurs for setting up cold storages across the country; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nwhether the Government has implemented any yojana to create storage facility in \nrural areas to prevent distress sale of their produce by the farmers and if so, the details \nthereof indicating the number of godowns set up along with the total capacity create\nd during \nthe last two years \nunder the scheme, State/UT\n-\nwise?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI NARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n \n(a) to (\ne\n):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT \nREFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (\ne\n) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n323\n  \nDUE FOR REPLY ON 16\nTH\n \nJULY, 2019.\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \n \nAs per a study on All India Cold\n-\nchain Infrastructure Capacity (AICIC\n-\n2015) \nconducted by NABCONS, it was reported that by 2014, t\nhere was cold storage capacity of \n32 million tonnes in the country against an approximate requirement of 35 million tonnes\n.\n \n \n \nAs per the latest available information received from States, there are 8038 cold \nstorages with capacity of 36.77 million tonnes in the country. The State \n–\n \nwise details are \nat \nAnnexure \n–\n \nI\n.\n \n \n \nFurther, Base line Survey conducted during December, 2013 by M/\ns Hansa \nResearch Group estimated that 92% of cold storage are owned and operated by private \nsector, 3% cooperative and remaining 5% are under Public Sector.\n \n \n \n \n \nFurther, Ministry of Consumer Affairs, Food & Public Distribution deals with the \nstorage of foo\ndgrains post procurement by Food Corporation of India (FCI) and State \nAgencies. Against total stocks of 741.41 LMT (as on 01.06.2019), the total storage \ncapacity available with Food Corporation of India (FCI), Central Warehousing Corporation \n(CWC) and the \nState Agencies (both owned and hired capacity), is 862.45 LMT (as on \n31.05.2019) comprising 739.76 LMT in covered godowns and 122.69 LMT in Cover and \nPlinth (CAP) storage. \n \n \n \nAs such, there is sufficient capacity for storage of central pool foodgrains at \nthe \nnational level.\n \n \n(c) & (d):\n \n \nThe Government is implementing the following schemes to provide \nincentives for setting up  of cold storages in the country:\n \n \ni\n)\n \nMission for Integrated Development of Horticulture (MIDH): \n \nDepartment of Agriculture Cooperation \n& Farmers Welfare is implementing MIDH \nunder which financial assistance is provided for various activities on horticulture \ndevelopment \n  \nfrom production to post harvest management \n  \nincluding establishment of \n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n \n\n-\n2\n-\n \ncold storage infrastructure. The component is demand/entrepreneur driven through \ncommercial ventures for which Government assistance in the form of credit linked back \nended subsidy is available. The assistance is available at the rate of 35% (for general \na\nreas) and 50%  (for hilly and scheduled areas) of capital cost of the project for both \npublic and private sector enterprises. During last two years 380 cold storages with \ncapacity of 1.75 million tonnes were approved with Rs. 623.84 crore assistance under \nMIDH (upto March 2019). The year \n–\n \nwise and State \n–\n \nwise details of cold storages \napproved under MIDH during the last two years are at \nAnnexure \n–\n \nII\n.  The allocations for \nMIDH Scheme as a whole which include allocation for cold storage activity for the cur\nrent \nyear are at \nAnnexure \n–\n \nIII. \nThe usage for cold storage activities under the scheme is as \nper demand of this activity in the respective States. \n \nii\n)\n \nPradhan Mantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana (PMKSY):\n \n \nMinistry of Food Processing Industries is implementing a Central Sector Scheme of \nIntegrated Cold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure (now subsumed under Pradhan \nMantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana) to provide integrated cold chain and preservation \ninfrastruct\nure facilities. Under this scheme, Ministry provides financial assistance in the \nform of grant\n-\nin\n-\naid at the rate of 35% for general area and 50% for North\n-\nEast States, \nHimalayan States, ITDP areas and Islands for storage and transport infrastructure subje\nct \nto a maximum grant\n-\nin\n-\naid of Rs. 10.00 crore for setting up integrated cold chain projects \nwith cold storage as one of the component. During last two years 160 cold chain projects \nwith capacity of 3.52 lakh MT were approved with Rs. 1273.37 crore assist\nance under \nPMKSY. \n \nThe year \n–\n \nwise and State \n–\n \nwise details of cold chain projects approved under \nthe scheme during the last two years is at \nAnnexure \n–\n \nIV\n.  \nDuring current year (2019 \n-\n \n20),  two cold chain projects with Rs. 12.24 crore as assistance under \nPMKSY have been \napproved in Andhra Pradesh and Karnataka.  \n \n \nContd…3/\n-\n \n \n\n \n-\n3\n-\n \n(e)\n:\n \n \nThe Government is promoting storage facilities in rural areas of the country \nthrough the scheme of “Agricultural Marketing Infrastructure (AMI)”, which is a sub\n-\nscheme under\n \nthe Integrated Scheme for Agricultural Marketing (ISAM). AMI scheme is a \nback ended capital subsidy scheme in which rate of subsidy provided is 25% and 33.33% \nbased on the category of eligible beneficiary and provided on capital cost of the project. \nAssis\ntance under the sub\n-\nscheme is available to individuals, groups of farmers/growers, \nregistered Farmer Produce Organizations (FPOs) etc. So far, a total of 38,964 storage \ninfrastructure projects (Godowns), with storage capacity of 655.48 Lakh MT have been \nsa\nnctioned across the country in rural areas. \n \n \nThe scheme is demand driven and during last two years, a total of 1699 storage \ninfrastructure projects (Godowns) have been assisted with storage capacity of 32.89 Lakh \nMT under storage component of AMI sub\n-\nsche\nme. The State\n-\nwise details are at \nAnnexure V\n. \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \n–\n \nI\n \n \nState \n-\n \nwise distribution of cold storages as on 31.03.2019\n \n \nName of state\n \nNumber\n \nCapacity  (MT)\n \nAndaman & Nicobar Islands (UT)\n \n3\n \n810\n \nAndhra Pradesh & Telangana\n \n458\n \n1867238\n \nArunachal \nPradesh\n \n2\n \n6000\n \nAssam\n \n38\n \n168096\n \nBihar\n \n309\n \n1437767\n \nChandigarh (UT)\n \n7\n \n12462\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n99\n \n487262\n \nDelhi \n \n97\n \n129857\n \nGoa\n \n29\n \n7705\n \nGujarat\n \n956\n \n3790311\n \nHaryana \n \n356\n \n819625\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n71\n \n135221\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n56\n \n188115\n \nJharkhan